---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { RANK_WEIGHTS } from '../lib/ranking.js';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '../lib/schema.js';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'How we rank', href: '/how-we-rank/' }];
const labels = {
  interventional: 'Interventional pain capability',
  rating: 'Verified patient rating',
  reviews: 'Verified review volume',
  tenure: 'Years in practice',
  transparency: 'Contact transparency',
};
const total = Object.values(RANK_WEIGHTS).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
const rows = Object.entries(RANK_WEIGHTS).map(([k, v]) => ({ label: labels[k], pct: Math.round((v / total) * 100) }));
---
<Base title="How we rank clinics"
      description="Our objective, published methodology for ranking pain clinics — board certification, interventional capability, verified reviews, and years in practice."
      jsonld={breadcrumbLd(crumbs)}>
  <div class="wrap narrow prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>How we rank clinics</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We rank clinics on objective, checkable signals — never on payment. Here is
      exactly what goes into the order you see.
    </p>

    <ul class="weights">
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <li>
          <span>{r.label}</span>
          <span class="bar"><span style={`width:${r.pct}%`}></span></span>
          <span class="pct">{r.pct}%</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>What each signal means</h2>
    <p><strong>Interventional pain capability.</strong> Whether the practice carries the
      interventional pain medicine credential (NUCC taxonomy 208VP0014X), indicating
      image-guided procedural capability beyond medication management.</p>
    <p><strong>Verified patient rating &amp; review volume.</strong> Aggregate rating and the
      number of verified reviews from public sources. Volume is scaled so a long track
      record counts without letting a single five-star review dominate.</p>
    <p><strong>Years in practice.</strong> Time since the practice's national provider
      record was established, capped so very established and moderately established
      practices aren't separated unfairly.</p>
    <p><strong>Contact transparency.</strong> Whether the clinic publishes a working
      website and phone number — a basic marker of accessibility.</p>

    <hr class="rule" />
    <h2>Who is ranked</h2>
    <p>
      A practice appears in the directory because the federal NPI registry shows a pain
      taxonomy among the codes it has declared. Some of those records are not places
      that treat pain — medical-supply and equipment companies, dispensing sites,
      laboratories, technologists. They stay listed, because the record is real and the
      address may be exactly what someone is looking for, but they take no rank among
      clinics that treat patients. You will find them below the ranked list on an area
      page, and filtering that page to their discipline ranks them against each other,
      which is the only comparison that means anything for them.
    </p>
    <p>
      That exclusion reads a practice's <em>primary</em> registration — the single code
      it registered itself under. A pain clinic that also dispenses braces is still a
      pain clinic and still ranks; only a record whose own registration says
      &ldquo;supplier&rdquo; is held out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every card names the practice type whenever pain medicine is not the practice's
      primary registration, so a chiropractor or a supplier says so before you call.
    </p>

    <hr class="rule" />
    <h2>Filtering by discipline</h2>
    <p>
      An area page can be narrowed to a discipline — chiropractic, physical therapy,
      podiatry, neurology, medical supply, and so on. Those chips read
      <em>every</em> taxonomy code a practice has declared, not just its primary one,
      because half the directory declares more than one: a practice registered as both
      an interventional pain provider and a chiropractor is findable under both, which
      is what someone searching for a chiropractor actually needs.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Only disciplines with at least one practice on that page are offered, and each
      chip carries its count, so you can see whether it will narrow anything before you
      spend a tap on it.
    </p>

    <hr class="rule" />
    <h2>Insurance</h2>
    <p>
      An area page can also be narrowed to clinics that accept Medicare, and that
      switch works alongside the discipline chips rather than replacing them — so
      &ldquo;chiropractic, accepts Medicare&rdquo; is a question you can ask.
    </p>
    <p>
      The signal comes from the federal CMS Medicare Fee-For-Service provider
      enrollment file, or from the clinic itself where it has claimed its listing
      and told us directly; the clinic's own answer always wins, because it knows
      its billing better than a registry lookup does.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A clinic missing from that federal file is never shown as refusing
      Medicare.</strong> A practice can bill under a group identifier, or have
      enrolled since the file was last refreshed, and we cannot tell those apart
      from genuine non-enrollment — so we mark only what we can confirm, and the
      filter offers no opposite. Whatever it says, check your specific plan with
      the clinic before you book.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      We do not publish commercial-plan networks. No free public source lists which
      practices are in-network for a given insurer, network status changes contract
      by contract, and a wrong answer there costs a patient real money — so until
      clinics tell us themselves, we would rather offer nothing than a guess.
    </p>

    <hr class="rule" />
    <h2>What we're still adding</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      As data coverage expands, we will fold in the range of procedures offered and
      the conditions each practice treats. Insurance and discipline are filters, not
      ranking signals — accepting Medicare has never moved a clinic up. We publish
      changes to this methodology rather than adjusting rankings silently.</p>

    <hr class="rule" />
    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>
      We do not accept payment in exchange for a higher ranking. Sponsored placements,
      if any, are clearly labeled and never alter the objective order above. The same
      goes for brand advertisements from businesses that are not clinics (a law firm,
      say): they are marked “Advertisement”, sit outside every ranked list, and have
      no say in what we write.
      {!SITE.disclosureActive && (
        <> Today, the organization behind this directory owns no clinics, so these
        rankings carry no ownership conflict.</>
      )}
      {' '}See our <a href="/ownership-disclosure/">ownership disclosure</a>.
    </p>
    {SITE.medicalReviewer.active && (
      <p class="muted">
        Reviewed by {SITE.medicalReviewer.name}, {SITE.medicalReviewer.credentials}.
      </p>
    )}
  </div>
</Base>
